WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.690
On a cheap string of fairy lights, one bulb dies and the whole string goes

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.350
dark.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.650
In your house, one bulb dies and every other light stays on.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:10.670
Pause.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.010
Which one is wired in series and which in parallel?

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.850
Why does it matter?

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.130
The fairy lights are in series.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.550
One break stops every bulb.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:24.850
Your house is in parallel.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.650
Each branch keeps its own full potential difference.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.890
Here are the two rules behind that.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.550
Series, one path.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.550
The same current, one amp, flows through both resistors, so the two ammeters

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:40.390
agree.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.030
The pds, potential differences, add up to the supply potential difference,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.270
4 volts plus 8 volts is 12 volts.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:50.490
Parallel.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.470
Each branch is connected across the same two points, so every branch has

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.370
the same potential difference, 12 volts each.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:02.010
The currents add, 3 amps plus 1 amp is 4 amps.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.490
The two parallel branches are 4 ohms and 12 ohms.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:13.370
1 over R P is 1 over 4 plus 1 over 12.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.390
That adds up to 1 over 3.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.550
Now invert on a new line.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.950
R P is 3 ohms.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.770
And the sense check.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.490
R P is always smaller than the smallest branch.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.470
3 is less than 4.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.710
Pause.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.210
The headlamp and the radio are both connected straight across an ideal battery.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:38.590
Switch on the radio.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.270
What happens to the headlamp?

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:43.770
It stays the same.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.850
It's still directly across the battery, so its pd and its current don't

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.390
change.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.970
The battery just supplies more current.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.110
The routine for any part of a circuit.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.670
Write every value on the diagram.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:04.050
Then V equals I times R for one component at a time, with its own V,

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.670
I and R.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.010
A 3 ohm bulb is in parallel with a 7 ohm resistor.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.010
Only one current is given.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.290
0,6 amps in the 7 ohm resistor.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.670
Pause.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.210
Why do I find V2 first?

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.270
Because the pd is the one thing both branches share.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.330
V equals I R, 0,6 times seven.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.210
That's 4,2 volts across the pair.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:41.930
Now R P, 1 over 3 plus 1 over 7, then invert, R P is 2,1 ohms.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:48.990
Now the bulb, its pd is 4,2 volts, 4,2 divided by 3, that's 1,4 amps.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:54.110
A1 reads the total, 1,4 plus 0,6, that's 2 amps.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:01.110
Now the check, 4,2 divided by R P, that's 4,2 divided by 2,1, it gives

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:01.830
2 amps.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.130
Two routes, one answer.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.390
Your turn.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.650
A 6 ohm resistor and a 4 ohm resistor are in parallel.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.570
The ammeter in the 6 ohm branch reads 0,5 amps.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:19.230
Find the pd across the pair, then R P, then the main line current and check

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:19.670
it.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:20.930
Pause and work it out.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.750
The pd is 0,5 times 6.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.230
That's 3 volts.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.190
R P is 2,4 ohms.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.630
The 4 ohm branch takes 3 divided by 4.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.550
That's 0,75 amps.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.790
Add the 0,5 amps in the other branch.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.450
The total is 1,25 amps.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:46.450
Check.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.710
3 divided by 2,4 is 1,25 amps.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:50.430
It matches.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.700
A network is a parallel group in series with something else.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.760
Work from the inside out.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:05.220
The group first, 10 ohms and 30 ohms in parallel, give 7,5 ohms.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:12.160
Then add the series part, 7,5 plus 4,5 is 12 ohms, that's the external resistance.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:17.200
The 3 ohms inside the battery box is for a later lesson.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.760
Then work back out.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.420
First, the total current.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.140
Next, the pd across the group.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.920
That current times R P.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.260
Last, each branch current.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.140
The group's pd divided by that branch's resistance.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:39.660
Every branch between P and Q has the same pd, 7,7 V.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.680
Along the lower branch, the pd adds up to that 7,7 V.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:47.460
They split in the ratio of the resistances.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:55.180
5,5 V across that branch's 10 ohm resistor, 2,2 V across its 4 ohm resistor.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.980
The battery is 24 volts and ideal.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.680
It supplies 60 watts.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.460
A 7 ohm resistor is in series with a parallel pair.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:09.820
The pair is a 3 ohm resistor and an unknown R.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:15.280
P equals V times I, so 60 equals 24 times the current.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.060
60 divided by 24, the current is 2,5 amps.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.400
Now the 7 ohm resistor.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:27.080
V equals I R, 2,5 times 7, that's 17,5 volts.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.080
Pause.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.960
Why does the voltmeter across the pair read 24, take away 17,5?

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.280
The 7 ohm resistor and the pair are in series across the battery, so their

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.100
pds add up to 24 volts.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.520
That leaves 6,5 volts for the pair.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.280
The 3 ohm resistor takes 6,5 divided by 3.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.940
That's 2,1667 amps.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.260
Keep it in the calculator.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:58.220
R gets the rest.

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.980
2,5 take away 2,1667.

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:04.380
That's a third of an amp.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:07.560
So R is 6,5 divided by a third.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.220
That's 19,5 ohms.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.800
The IEB memo rounds to 0,33 amps first.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.220
It prints 19,7 ohms.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.000
Keep the full value and you get 19,5 ohms.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.580
Round only at the end.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:24.650
Your turn.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.830
This one is from the IEB May 2024 paper.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.290
The switch is closed.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.070
V2 is across the 18 ohm resistor.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:33.570
It reads 6 volts.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.190
Find the reading on A1.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.630
Then the resistance of the parallel pair.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:40.030
Then the reading on A2.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.470
You don't need the battery's internal resistance.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.030
Pause and work it out.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.090
A1 is 6 divided by 18.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.090
That's a third of an amp.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:54.190
Keep it exact.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.750
The parallel pair is 14 ohms and 12 ohms.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.190
Its R P is 6,46 ohms.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.870
Keep 6,4615 in the calculator.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:10.270
The pd across the pair is a third, times 6,4615.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.010
That's 2,15 volts.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.890
A2 is that pd divided by 12.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.050
That's 0,18 amps.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:23.780
Next in the app is See it happen, probe a network with the voltmeter in

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.600
the Circuits Studio, then train, then check.

